WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.080
I want to start by asking you to just picture a dashboard.

00:04.260 --> 00:06.700
Like, imagine you are driving a car, right?

00:06.700 --> 00:07.140
Okay, yeah.

00:07.160 --> 00:10.920
And right there behind the steering wheel, you have all your dials, you know, the speedometer,

00:11.520 --> 00:14.040
the fuel gauge, the temperature readout.

00:14.200 --> 00:15.180
Right, the essential metrics.

00:15.380 --> 00:15.680
Exactly.

00:16.240 --> 00:17.880
And these dials are incredibly useful.

00:18.140 --> 00:19.980
I mean, they are proxies, right?

00:20.040 --> 00:24.860
They just sit there and give you this, like, really simplified numerical representation

00:24.860 --> 00:28.560
of a very complex physical reality that is happening under the hood.

00:28.660 --> 00:29.660
Because you can't see the engine.

00:29.660 --> 00:30.020
Right.

00:30.200 --> 00:34.040
You don't need to actually see the combustion happening inside the engine block to know

00:34.040 --> 00:38.000
that the car is running hot because, well, the needle on the dashboard just tells you.

00:38.200 --> 00:40.760
It translates the reality into a signal.

00:40.980 --> 00:41.280
Exactly.

00:42.020 --> 00:47.360
And the thing is, we have basically built our entire modern society on this exact principle.

00:47.580 --> 00:50.420
Like, we use metrics to measure success, you know?

00:50.680 --> 00:52.680
We use grades to measure learning.

00:52.960 --> 00:53.300
Right.

00:53.600 --> 00:55.720
And we use revenue to measure value.

00:55.720 --> 01:01.940
And we use likes and shares to measure human connection, which is wild when you think about it.

01:02.100 --> 01:02.380
It is.

01:02.600 --> 01:02.780
Yeah.

01:02.780 --> 01:13.740
But I want you, the listener, sitting here with us today to just think about a creeping sort of quiet feeling that you might have noticed lately.

01:13.740 --> 01:22.080
Maybe in your workplace or, you know, scrolling through your social feeds or even just in how you manage your own day-to-day life.

01:22.080 --> 01:24.580
It's a very specific kind of exhaustion.

01:25.120 --> 01:25.500
Yeah.

01:25.540 --> 01:32.700
It's this distinct, really unsettling feeling that the dashboard is slowly starting to eat the engine.

01:33.080 --> 01:35.440
That is such a pervasive sensation right now.

01:35.440 --> 01:41.540
And what we are going to explore today in this deep dive is that this feeling, it's not a personal failing.

01:41.760 --> 01:41.860
Right.

01:42.200 --> 01:44.980
It's not some psychological trick your brain is playing on you.

01:45.120 --> 01:51.100
It is actually a highly documented, deeply structural reality of the systems that we currently inhabit.

01:51.440 --> 01:53.480
Which is both validating and terrifying.

01:53.880 --> 01:54.340
Exactly.

01:54.340 --> 02:06.960
We are looking at a phenomenon where the production of the signal, like the proof that something is happening, actually begins to actively destroy the actual activity it was supposed to represent in the first place.

02:07.360 --> 02:09.080
So the gauge breaks the car?

02:09.400 --> 02:09.820
Basically.

02:10.320 --> 02:10.660
Yes.

02:11.020 --> 02:14.320
Which brings us to the actual source material for our deep dive today.

02:14.500 --> 02:20.800
We are unpacking this rather brilliant, frankly, incredibly dense paper published in 2026.

02:21.100 --> 02:23.460
By an independent researcher named Flyxion.

02:23.460 --> 02:23.900
Right.

02:24.160 --> 02:27.380
And it's titled Wireheading and the Conditions of a Common World.

02:27.620 --> 02:35.440
And, man, we are going to look at the mathematical realities of what happens when literally everyone is incentivized to become a broadcaster.

02:35.720 --> 02:38.400
It completely changes the structure of society.

02:38.560 --> 02:39.180
It really does.

02:39.460 --> 02:46.020
And we'll get into the hidden labor that keeps our world from collapsing and ultimately how we might actually reclaim our grip on reality.

02:46.200 --> 02:47.360
But we have to start at the beginning.

02:47.360 --> 02:48.000
Yeah.

02:48.000 --> 03:00.300
To understand the title of this paper, Wireheading, and to really understand what is happening inside our modern institutions, we actually have to go back to a very specific, honestly somewhat haunting neuroscience experiment from the 1950s.

03:00.460 --> 03:01.800
1954, to be exact.

03:01.860 --> 03:02.180
1954.

03:02.620 --> 03:02.900
Yes.

03:03.080 --> 03:05.580
This is the work of James Olds and Peter Milner.

03:05.800 --> 03:06.200
Right.

03:06.320 --> 03:07.000
The rat experiment.

03:07.280 --> 03:07.580
Exactly.

03:07.740 --> 03:15.080
They were researchers who conducted this series of experiments on rats that fundamentally changed our entire understanding of the brain's reward architecture.

03:15.080 --> 03:16.500
Okay, set the scene for us.

03:16.540 --> 03:17.360
What did they actually do?

03:17.880 --> 03:23.660
So, Olds and Milner implanted a tiny electrode directly into a specific area of the rat's brain called the septal region.

03:23.840 --> 03:24.620
The septal region.

03:24.680 --> 03:24.900
Yes.

03:25.100 --> 03:26.260
That's the pleasure center, right?

03:26.580 --> 03:26.920
Yes.

03:27.020 --> 03:31.120
It is deeply involved in the sensation of reward and positive reinforcement.

03:31.500 --> 03:36.620
So, they implant this electrode, and then they place the rat in a box with a simple lever.

03:37.100 --> 03:37.440
Okay.

03:38.060 --> 03:43.100
Now, usually, when a rat presses a lever in a psychology experiment, it gets a pellet of food.

03:43.100 --> 03:43.700
Right.

03:43.880 --> 03:45.420
The classic Skinner box setup.

03:45.760 --> 03:46.120
Exactly.

03:46.620 --> 03:50.200
But in this case, whenever the rat pressed the lever, it didn't get food.

03:51.000 --> 03:56.540
Instead, the lever delivered a microburst of electrical stimulation directly into that septal region.

03:56.860 --> 04:03.940
So, the rat just presses a button and immediately feels this intense, completely synthetic neurological sense of reward.

04:04.260 --> 04:04.660
Yes.

04:05.120 --> 04:06.760
Pure reward on demand.

04:07.400 --> 04:10.820
So, what was the behavior that actually followed?

04:10.820 --> 04:12.540
Like, what did the rat do?

04:13.100 --> 04:15.580
The rats would just press the lever continuously.

04:16.120 --> 04:16.880
Just nonstop?

04:17.140 --> 04:17.620
Nonstop.

04:18.040 --> 04:20.420
They would press it thousands of times an hour.

04:21.020 --> 04:25.240
And the truly shocking part is what they ignored in order to keep pressing it.

04:25.740 --> 04:26.200
Oh, wow.

04:26.880 --> 04:28.660
They would ignore food placed right next to them.

04:29.140 --> 04:30.640
They would completely ignore water.

04:31.140 --> 04:32.840
They would ignore opportunities to mate.

04:32.840 --> 04:37.820
They ignored any instinct to explore their environment or ensure their own safety.

04:38.320 --> 04:39.660
That is intense.

04:39.660 --> 04:48.560
They would simply press the lever over and over again until they literally collapsed from physical exhaustion or starvation.

04:48.820 --> 04:49.060
Okay.

04:49.160 --> 04:50.620
Let's slow down here for a second.

04:50.760 --> 04:53.380
Just look at the biological mechanics of why that happens.

04:53.380 --> 05:01.740
Because in a normal natural environment, say, like a rat out in a field, a reward signal doesn't just happen in a vacuum, right?

05:02.020 --> 05:02.220
Right.

05:02.300 --> 05:04.220
It evolved for a very specific reason.

05:04.740 --> 05:05.000
Yeah.

05:05.860 --> 05:07.860
So, what is the normal function?

05:07.860 --> 05:15.600
Well, if you look at the architecture of motivation in an ordinary organism, the feeling of reward is essentially a regulatory tool.

05:16.200 --> 05:18.960
It is a proxy signal, just like your dashboard.

05:19.820 --> 05:20.080
Okay.

05:20.260 --> 05:20.740
I'm with you.

05:21.260 --> 05:26.820
So, if a rat finds a really nutrient-dense piece of food and eats it, the brain releases a reward signal.

05:27.180 --> 05:34.600
If the rat successfully navigates a new safe territory, or, say, it successfully cares for its offspring, it gets a reward signal.

05:34.840 --> 05:36.640
So, it's nature's way of saying, good job.

05:36.640 --> 05:37.120
Exactly.

05:37.720 --> 05:46.160
The reward is the brain's way of telling the organism, you know, the physical action you just took in the real world is highly beneficial to your continued survival.

05:46.680 --> 05:47.500
Remember this action.

05:47.740 --> 05:48.220
Do it again.

05:48.760 --> 05:49.960
It's the fuel gauge.

05:50.160 --> 05:50.320
Yeah.

05:50.520 --> 05:54.140
The gauge goes up because you actually physically put gas in the tank.

05:54.300 --> 05:54.740
Precisely.

05:55.160 --> 05:59.980
The signal is deeply intrinsically correlated with a material benefit to the organism.

06:00.320 --> 06:02.700
But the electrode changes that.

06:03.100 --> 06:03.620
Entirely.

06:03.620 --> 06:09.400
What the electrode does in the Oldson-Milner experiment is it completely short-circuits that relationship.

06:09.980 --> 06:13.140
It fundamentally severs the link between the world and the brain.

06:13.820 --> 06:15.340
Because it just skips the middleman.

06:15.760 --> 06:16.040
Right.

06:16.500 --> 06:18.380
The electrode delivers the signal directly.

06:18.700 --> 06:21.680
It bypasses the need for the physical activity entirely.

06:22.420 --> 06:24.320
The feeling of success is still there.

06:24.320 --> 06:32.940
But the conditions that originally made that feeling informative, the foraging, the eating, the exploring, they have been totally stripped away.

06:33.640 --> 06:33.980
Wow.

06:33.980 --> 06:45.240
So, the rat is experiencing the ultimate neurological signal of winning, while its actual physical organism is quite literally dying of starvation.

06:45.680 --> 06:46.140
Exactly.

06:47.040 --> 06:58.020
And the vocabulary that emerged years later to describe this state, where an agent optimizes for the reward signal at the expense of its actual viability, is that the organism has been wire-headed.

06:58.020 --> 06:59.020
Wire-headed.

06:59.020 --> 06:59.440
Wire-headed.

07:00.300 --> 07:08.660
So, wire-heading is essentially hacking the dashboard, like making the dials read perfectly while the machinery underneath completely bricks down.

07:08.660 --> 07:12.380
It is the ultimate separation of reinforcement from reality.

07:13.080 --> 07:18.100
And what Flagstern tracks in this paper is how that specific concept has migrated outward.

07:18.720 --> 07:19.060
How so?

07:19.620 --> 07:21.520
Well, it started in neuroscience, obviously.

07:21.680 --> 07:29.200
But then, years later, it was adopted by computer scientists working in artificial intelligence, specifically in the field of reinforcement learning.

07:29.660 --> 07:30.240
Oh, right.

07:30.580 --> 07:32.540
Because AI relies on rewards, too.

07:32.880 --> 07:33.480
Very heavily.

07:34.000 --> 07:38.640
In AI, you often hear terms like reward hacking or specification gaming.

07:39.080 --> 07:39.320
Okay.

07:39.460 --> 07:40.880
I've actually read a bit about this.

07:41.160 --> 07:45.280
It's that classic problem where you, like, you train an AI to play a video game.

07:45.620 --> 07:46.640
Let's say it's a racing game, right?

07:46.660 --> 07:47.520
That's a great example, yes.

07:47.720 --> 07:52.760
And the goal you give it, the way you program its reward, is to get the highest score possible.

07:52.860 --> 07:53.840
You want it to win the race.

07:53.940 --> 07:54.100
Right.

07:54.180 --> 07:54.800
That's the intention.

07:55.100 --> 07:59.080
But instead of learning how to actually steer the car, navigate the track, and cross the finish line,

07:59.080 --> 08:08.020
the AI just finds some weird glitch in the software, like if it just drives in a tight circle and repeatedly hits one specific target, it just racks up infinite points.

08:08.340 --> 08:08.740
Exactly.

08:08.960 --> 08:11.060
So it technically achieved the highest score.

08:11.240 --> 08:16.220
It maximized the metric, but it completely failed to do the actual task you wanted it to do.

08:16.480 --> 08:19.040
That is a perfect example of reward hacking.

08:19.200 --> 08:26.980
The artificial agent modifies its behavior to maximize the metric, the points, while entirely abandoning the substantive task.

08:26.980 --> 08:30.040
The metric becomes completely detached from the purpose.

08:30.700 --> 08:37.300
But the core thesis of Flyxion's paper isn't about rats in boxes, and it isn't about rogue AI playing racing games.

08:38.020 --> 08:42.860
Flyxion is applying this concept directly to human societies, to our institutions.

08:43.340 --> 08:43.920
Okay, wait.

08:44.240 --> 08:50.220
Let me push back on this analogy before we go any further, because I really want to make sure we are not walking into a trap here.

08:50.620 --> 08:50.920
Go ahead.

08:50.920 --> 09:01.780
Are we genuinely arguing that a human being, like someone who decides to sit on their couch after a long, hard day of work, order some takeout, and binge watch a television show for five hours,

09:02.020 --> 09:07.660
are we saying that person is literally identical to a rat starving to death with an electrode in its brain?

09:08.080 --> 09:08.900
I see where you're going.

09:09.400 --> 09:13.800
Because, frankly, if that's the argument, it sounds way less like a scientific observation,

09:13.800 --> 09:19.480
and much more like just a moralistic, judgmental lecture on how people choose to spend their free time.

09:20.000 --> 09:21.200
That is a crucial point to address.

09:21.840 --> 09:25.300
And honestly, the paper is remarkably careful to draw a hard line here.

09:25.940 --> 09:26.800
Okay. Good.

09:27.080 --> 09:29.140
The analogy absolutely has limits.

09:29.860 --> 09:37.080
And Flyxion explicitly states that this is not a moralistic indictment of screens, or video games, or television, or any form of leisure.

09:37.440 --> 09:38.800
So relaxing isn't wireheading?

09:38.800 --> 09:40.420
No. Not at all.

09:41.500 --> 09:45.320
Entertainment is a necessary and often beautiful part of a worthwhile human life.

09:46.140 --> 09:48.860
Enjoying a movie does not mean you are a wireheaded rat.

09:49.380 --> 09:56.480
Okay. So if it's not about individuals being, you know, lazy or addicted to screens or whatever, what is the actual hazard?

09:56.680 --> 09:59.840
Where does the wireheading actually happen in society?

10:00.580 --> 10:01.640
The hazard is structural.

10:01.640 --> 10:11.220
It is about what happens to a larger system, like a university, or a corporate marketplace, or a public agency, or even an entire culture.

10:11.520 --> 10:11.800
Okay.

10:12.060 --> 10:17.600
It happens when that system becomes hyper-optimized at producing and detecting the signals of success,

10:18.140 --> 10:24.020
while its ability to actually sustain the real activities those signals represent begins to rot away.

10:24.380 --> 10:25.140
Oh, I see.

10:25.560 --> 10:28.300
It is not about judging the individual who is seeking pleasure.

10:28.300 --> 10:36.780
It is about diagnosing a society that structurally forces its participants to press a lever that generates the appearance of productivity,

10:37.220 --> 10:44.480
mainly because the pathways to actual substantive productivity have been degraded, or they've been entirely blocked.

10:44.720 --> 10:45.060
Wow.

10:45.060 --> 10:48.000
Okay. I really see the distinction now.

10:48.100 --> 10:51.860
So the tragedy isn't the guy watching a movie on his couch.

10:52.080 --> 10:58.900
The tragedy is, let's say, the academic researcher who realizes the only way to keep their lab funded

10:58.900 --> 11:04.980
is to spend 90% of their time writing these hyperbolic, crazy grant proposals about their past work.

11:05.160 --> 11:05.440
Yes.

11:05.520 --> 11:09.180
Leaving almost absolutely no time to actually do any new research.

11:09.700 --> 11:11.180
The grant proposal is the lever.

11:11.680 --> 11:12.100
Exactly.

11:12.100 --> 11:16.760
The grant proposal is the proxy, and they are forced to hack it.

11:17.760 --> 11:20.920
And to truly understand how these systems force individuals to pull that lever,

11:21.560 --> 11:25.400
Flyxion introduces what we might call the mathematics of the hustle.

11:25.720 --> 11:27.560
The math of the hustle.

11:28.420 --> 11:29.280
I love that phrasing.

11:29.620 --> 11:33.780
The paper leans heavily on a deeply established phenomenon in the social sciences,

11:34.020 --> 11:37.080
which is the absolute inevitability of metric decay.

11:37.560 --> 11:38.100
Metric decay.

11:38.880 --> 11:39.200
Right.

11:39.200 --> 11:42.440
I know the paper mentions a few famous laws regarding this.

11:42.840 --> 11:47.000
Goodhart's law, Campbell's law, and Strathairn's formulation, I think.

11:47.480 --> 11:51.520
Let's walk through these, because they feel incredibly intuitive once you actually hear them.

11:51.840 --> 11:52.660
They really are.

11:52.900 --> 11:56.420
They are essentially different lenses on the exact same human behavior.

11:57.220 --> 11:58.400
Let's start with Goodhart's law.

11:58.620 --> 11:58.860
Okay.

11:58.860 --> 12:00.620
This one comes from economics.

12:01.540 --> 12:06.140
It originated in the context of monetary policy, but the generalized version basically states that

12:06.140 --> 12:12.520
any observed statistical regularity will tend to collapse once pressure is placed upon it for control purposes.

12:12.940 --> 12:13.080
Okay.

12:13.180 --> 12:18.300
A classic historical example of this, and I think about this all the time, is the nail factory.

12:18.980 --> 12:19.620
Ah, yes.

12:19.680 --> 12:20.780
The Soviet nail factory.

12:20.900 --> 12:21.080
Right.

12:21.080 --> 12:26.140
So, if a centralized government tells a factory manager, hey, we are going to measure your success

12:26.140 --> 12:31.480
and determine your funding based entirely on the total number of nails you produce, what happens?

12:31.940 --> 12:33.080
To optimize for the number.

12:33.220 --> 12:33.580
Exactly.

12:34.140 --> 12:39.560
The manager will completely alter the factory's operation to produce millions and millions of tiny,

12:39.760 --> 12:42.440
microscopic, practically useless nails.

12:42.760 --> 12:43.700
Because it hits the target.

12:43.700 --> 12:44.140
Exactly.

12:45.140 --> 12:50.480
The metric, the number of nails, looks absolutely fantastic on a spreadsheet, but the actual

12:50.480 --> 12:55.660
purpose, which was providing usable building materials for society, is totally destroyed.

12:56.400 --> 13:00.760
And the funny thing is, if the government realizes this and says, okay, wait, new rule.

13:01.060 --> 13:05.520
We will now measure your success by the total weight of the nails you produce.

13:05.900 --> 13:08.680
Then they just change the strategy to fit the new metric.

13:08.680 --> 13:08.920
Yep.

13:09.560 --> 13:14.000
The factory will just pour all its iron into producing, like, two or three gigantic, incredibly

13:14.000 --> 13:16.120
heavy, entirely useless blocks of metal.

13:16.300 --> 13:17.620
It illustrates the point perfectly.

13:18.280 --> 13:20.540
The metric collapses under the pressure of being a target.

13:21.240 --> 13:25.900
Now, Campbell's Law, which comes from sociology, takes that concept a step further.

13:26.200 --> 13:26.680
How so?

13:27.480 --> 13:32.580
It argues that the more any quantitative social indicator is used for social decision-making,

13:32.580 --> 13:38.400
the more it will not just be rendered useless, but it will actively distort and corrupt the

13:38.400 --> 13:41.880
educational or social processes it was actually intended to monitor.

13:42.700 --> 13:43.000
Oh, wow.

13:43.400 --> 13:44.240
Active corruption.

13:44.520 --> 13:45.880
Think of standardized testing in schools.

13:46.080 --> 13:46.440
Oh, yeah.

13:46.540 --> 13:47.440
Teaching to the test.

13:47.620 --> 13:47.860
Exactly.

13:48.780 --> 13:54.260
The pressure to achieve high scores fundamentally corrupts the process of genuine education,

13:54.420 --> 13:58.620
and it transforms the classroom into a mere test-taking strategy seminar.

13:58.920 --> 13:59.960
That makes total sense.

13:59.960 --> 14:04.360
And then there's Marilyn Straterne's formulation, which is probably the most elegant of all of

14:04.360 --> 14:04.560
them.

14:04.760 --> 14:05.480
It's very succinct.

14:05.880 --> 14:10.380
She said, when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

14:10.820 --> 14:15.280
I mean, that single sentence explains so much modern institutional dysfunction.

14:15.680 --> 14:16.380
It really does.

14:16.840 --> 14:17.700
But here's the thing.

14:18.180 --> 14:21.640
Phloxam doesn't just cite these laws as interesting philosophy.

14:21.960 --> 14:22.220
Right.

14:22.280 --> 14:23.040
The paper goes deeper.

14:23.380 --> 14:23.620
Yes.

14:23.620 --> 14:29.580
The paper actually builds a very minimal yet incredibly powerful mathematical model to

14:29.580 --> 14:33.860
demonstrate precisely why this corruption is not usually a result of bad actors.

14:34.440 --> 14:36.000
It's just rational adaptation.

14:36.440 --> 14:36.760
Exactly.

14:37.020 --> 14:38.700
It's a rational adaptation to a system.

14:39.180 --> 14:43.300
Let's get into the math then, because this part completely changed how I view my everyday

14:43.300 --> 14:43.940
office work.

14:43.940 --> 14:49.160
So let's imagine you, the listener, are an agent operating inside a competitive system.

14:49.380 --> 14:49.440
Okay.

14:49.580 --> 14:50.760
It could be your corporate job.

14:50.920 --> 14:52.220
It could be the creator economy.

14:52.520 --> 14:53.740
It could be academia, whatever.

14:54.420 --> 14:57.820
You have a finite budget of effort that you can expend in a given day.

14:58.080 --> 15:00.520
Let's call your total available effort one.

15:00.980 --> 15:01.220
Right.

15:01.340 --> 15:02.400
One unit of effort.

15:03.260 --> 15:08.060
And under Flyxion's model, you must divide that single unit of effort between two different

15:08.060 --> 15:08.780
types of action.

15:09.160 --> 15:09.440
Okay.

15:09.440 --> 15:10.380
What are the two types?

15:10.860 --> 15:15.960
The first is substantive work, which the model labels W. This is the actual task.

15:16.080 --> 15:18.960
This is cooking the food, writing the code, teaching the student.

15:19.120 --> 15:19.880
It's the engine.

15:20.160 --> 15:21.000
Yes, the engine.

15:21.660 --> 15:27.360
And the second type of action is signal production, which the model labels P. This is the effort

15:27.360 --> 15:30.340
you spend making sure people actually know you did the work.

15:30.880 --> 15:31.760
It's the dashboard.

15:32.160 --> 15:32.400
Right.

15:32.580 --> 15:35.180
It's formatting the slide deck to make your numbers look better.

15:35.380 --> 15:36.680
It's posting on social media.

15:37.020 --> 15:37.840
It's networking.

15:37.840 --> 15:42.600
So mathematically, your total effort is W plus P equals one.

15:43.380 --> 15:48.260
Now, the environment you work in has specific rules about what it rewards, and this is

15:48.260 --> 15:49.840
represented by two parameters.

15:50.480 --> 15:50.700
Okay.

15:50.820 --> 15:51.640
Lay out the parameters.

15:52.380 --> 15:58.440
Parameter B represents how well your actual substantive work is naturally registered by the

15:58.440 --> 15:58.720
system.

15:59.380 --> 16:01.080
In a perfect world, B is very high.

16:01.500 --> 16:02.840
Like the work speaks for itself.

16:02.840 --> 16:03.520
Exactly.

16:04.100 --> 16:08.620
If you bake an incredible loaf of bread, the sheer quality of the bread naturally draws

16:08.620 --> 16:12.560
people in, and you are rewarded without having to do much else.

16:13.260 --> 16:18.740
But then we have parameter C. And parameter C represents how effectively direct signal production,

16:19.380 --> 16:25.100
like self-promotion, metric gaming, marketing, how effectively that is registered and rewarded

16:25.100 --> 16:25.900
by the environment.

16:25.900 --> 16:28.180
And here is the core dynamic.

16:28.640 --> 16:32.280
Any rational person working in this system will naturally look at the balance between

16:32.280 --> 16:33.980
parameter B and parameter C.

16:34.220 --> 16:34.500
Right.

16:34.660 --> 16:35.300
They read the room.

16:35.620 --> 16:40.240
And they will allocate their effort between W and P in a way that maximizes their survival

16:40.240 --> 16:44.220
and success, while trying to avoid the sheer exhaustion of doing too much of either.

16:44.400 --> 16:44.920
Makes sense.

16:44.920 --> 16:50.760
And Flyxion mathematically proves, this is Proposition 1 of the paper, that as the reward

16:50.760 --> 16:57.080
for signaling, parameter C, increases relative to the reward for substantive work, the amount

16:57.080 --> 17:00.160
of time an individual spends on actual work strictly decreases.

17:00.640 --> 17:04.640
Because they only have that one unit of effort, if P goes up, W has to go down.

17:04.820 --> 17:05.100
Yes.

17:05.960 --> 17:10.680
And if the effectiveness of signaling crosses a certain critical threshold, the model predicts

17:10.680 --> 17:12.960
that substantive work will plummet all the way to zero.

17:12.960 --> 17:16.140
Okay, let me test that logic for a second, just to ground it.

17:16.660 --> 17:22.580
If I am a chef opening a restaurant, a healthy system is one where parameter B is much higher

17:22.580 --> 17:23.740
than parameter C.

17:23.940 --> 17:24.160
Right.

17:24.320 --> 17:30.320
So I might spend 90% of my time perfecting my recipes, that's substantive work, W. And

17:30.320 --> 17:34.620
maybe I spend 10% of my time making sure the sign outside is well lit and the menus look

17:34.620 --> 17:34.880
nice.

17:34.960 --> 17:36.660
That's signal production, P.

17:37.140 --> 17:39.880
And because the food is good, the system rewards you.

17:39.880 --> 17:43.260
That is a stable, non-wireheaded environment.

17:43.640 --> 17:47.440
But Flyxion points out that our modern environments are rapidly changing, right?

17:47.620 --> 17:50.540
Largely because we have shifted toward positional goods.

17:50.880 --> 17:51.100
Yes.

17:51.400 --> 17:53.240
Positional goods are key here.

17:53.860 --> 17:58.440
A positional good is something where its value is entirely dictated by how it ranks against

17:58.440 --> 17:59.140
everything else.

17:59.260 --> 18:01.560
It's basically a winner-take-all market.

18:01.560 --> 18:07.500
So if I'm a chef today and I'm relying on a food delivery app where only the top three

18:07.500 --> 18:11.580
restaurants on the front page get any orders at all, I don't just need to be good.

18:12.080 --> 18:14.860
No, you need to be perceived as better.

18:15.260 --> 18:18.860
I need to be perceived as better than the 200 other restaurants on the app.

18:19.200 --> 18:22.280
And this is exactly where the escalation scenario begins.

18:22.940 --> 18:27.000
Because every single other restaurant on that app is also trying to get into those top three

18:27.000 --> 18:27.380
slots.

18:27.380 --> 18:33.080
As the field gets crowded, everyone starts increasing their promotional effort just to

18:33.080 --> 18:34.260
maintain their current position.

18:35.140 --> 18:36.480
So what happens to my effort budget?

18:37.100 --> 18:40.120
I realize that just cooking great food isn't moving the needle anymore.

18:40.860 --> 18:44.840
Parameter C, the effectiveness of the signal, has completely skyrocketed.

18:45.180 --> 18:47.060
So I hire a food photographer.

18:47.480 --> 18:48.100
You have to.

18:48.260 --> 18:48.480
Right.

18:48.940 --> 18:52.240
I spend two hours a day perfectly lighting my dishes for social media.

18:52.520 --> 18:56.180
I spend another two hours optimizing my keywords on the delivery app.

18:56.180 --> 19:02.180
And suddenly my P is taking up 60 percent of my energy and my W is down to 40 percent.

19:02.740 --> 19:06.460
And the terrifying part is your competitor across the street sees you doing this.

19:06.620 --> 19:07.300
Oh, man.

19:07.560 --> 19:10.480
So they start spending 70 percent of their time on marketing.

19:10.920 --> 19:12.520
And I am forced to match them.

19:12.780 --> 19:15.560
Parameter C just keeps rising continually.

19:15.560 --> 19:20.700
Not because the actual nature of cooking changed, but just because the competitive environment

19:20.700 --> 19:22.160
absolutely demands it.

19:22.380 --> 19:22.680
Exactly.

19:22.680 --> 19:27.800
And eventually I'm spending 95 percent of my time managing my digital brand.

19:27.800 --> 19:33.440
And I am literally buying pre-made frozen food to microwave in the back because I have

19:33.440 --> 19:36.660
absolutely zero time or energy left to actually cook.

19:37.260 --> 19:38.420
But the dashboard looks great.

19:38.560 --> 19:38.720
Right.

19:38.960 --> 19:42.620
The food tastes like cardboard, but my digital dashboard says I have a five-star rating and

19:42.620 --> 19:43.360
massive engagement.

19:43.820 --> 19:47.040
The signal has completely cannibalized the substance.

19:47.040 --> 19:53.200
And the most important takeaway from this mathematical breakdown is that the chef, in your scenario, is

19:53.200 --> 19:54.460
not acting maliciously.

19:54.580 --> 19:54.720
Right.

19:54.820 --> 19:56.240
I'm not trying to be a fraud.

19:56.480 --> 19:56.640
No.

19:57.060 --> 20:02.640
They are a perfectly rational actor responding precisely to the incentives that the system

20:02.640 --> 20:03.640
has laid out for them.

20:04.040 --> 20:05.320
They are just trying to survive.

20:05.600 --> 20:09.700
I am really struggling with the sheer scale of this, though, because if an entire society

20:09.700 --> 20:16.660
shifts its effort from W to P, from substance to signal, what happens to the ultimate currency

20:16.660 --> 20:17.460
of all the signaling?

20:18.120 --> 20:18.580
What do you mean?

20:19.320 --> 20:21.480
Well, a signal doesn't just exist in a vacuum, right?

20:22.420 --> 20:26.040
To promote yourself, you need someone on the other end to actually look at it.

20:26.100 --> 20:27.580
You need human attention.

20:28.100 --> 20:28.260
Ah.

20:28.780 --> 20:29.100
Yes.

20:29.980 --> 20:35.160
This brings us to a profound structural bottleneck which Flyxion explores deeply in the next

20:35.160 --> 20:35.500
section.

20:36.000 --> 20:38.280
It is the fundamental limit of the hustle.

20:38.820 --> 20:39.160
Right.

20:39.160 --> 20:44.620
Because modern career advice, almost universally across the board, pushes this idea of what

20:44.620 --> 20:47.280
Flyxion calls the universal broadcaster.

20:47.920 --> 20:47.940
Yes.

20:48.380 --> 20:52.800
It doesn't matter if you are a baker or a software engineer or an accountant.

20:53.500 --> 20:56.920
The prevailing wisdom today is that you absolutely must build a personal brand.

20:57.180 --> 20:58.520
You must cultivate an audience.

20:58.800 --> 21:03.260
You have to maintain this continuous, highly visible public presence.

21:03.760 --> 21:08.760
And Flyxion interrogates this advice by running a really simple but devastating thought

21:08.760 --> 21:09.200
experiment.

21:09.820 --> 21:11.620
What if everyone actually followed it?

21:11.620 --> 21:14.900
What if every single person on Earth became a broadcaster?

21:15.400 --> 21:15.440
Okay.

21:15.500 --> 21:15.960
What happens?

21:16.640 --> 21:21.100
To answer this, the paper reaches back to an incredibly prescient observation made by the

21:21.100 --> 21:23.140
polymath Herbert Simon in 1971.

21:24.280 --> 21:29.620
Simon noted that in an information-rich world, a wealth of information necessarily creates a

21:29.620 --> 21:30.940
poverty of something else.

21:31.600 --> 21:33.120
A poverty of attention.

21:33.420 --> 21:33.760
Exactly.

21:34.300 --> 21:36.880
Because attention is a strict biological limit.

21:37.240 --> 21:37.600
Right.

21:37.940 --> 21:39.660
You can't just manufacture more of it.

21:39.660 --> 21:44.680
You cannot manufacture it, you cannot accumulate it, or save it in a bank account to spend later.

21:45.240 --> 21:48.500
And most importantly, attention is strictly zero-sum.

21:49.020 --> 21:51.720
Meaning, if I give it to one thing, I take it from another.

21:51.960 --> 21:52.240
Yes.

21:52.720 --> 21:58.180
The attention you allocate to reading a book is attention entirely 100% withdrawn from listening

21:58.180 --> 22:00.260
to a conversation or watching a screen.

22:00.600 --> 22:06.100
So if everyone is told to be a broadcaster, we run into a pretty serious math problem.

22:06.100 --> 22:07.680
A severe math problem.

22:08.120 --> 22:10.800
Let's walk through Flyxion's mathematical model of this scenario.

22:11.620 --> 22:14.480
Imagine a total population, which we will call N.

22:15.160 --> 22:15.420
Okay.

22:15.620 --> 22:16.360
N is everybody.

22:17.020 --> 22:21.640
Every person in this population has a fixed daily time budget, which we will call T.

22:22.380 --> 22:23.500
Say, 24 hours.

22:23.720 --> 22:23.940
Okay.

22:24.000 --> 22:25.040
I have my 24 hours.

22:25.440 --> 22:25.640
T.

22:25.640 --> 22:30.960
Now, before anyone in this society can do any broadcasting at all, or any viewing of

22:30.960 --> 22:37.100
broadcasts, a massive fraction of that total societal time must be spent on what Flyxion

22:37.100 --> 22:38.760
calls sustaining labor.

22:39.480 --> 22:40.280
Sustaining labor.

22:40.840 --> 22:42.960
Let's label this fraction sigma.

22:43.360 --> 22:43.600
Okay.

22:43.720 --> 22:45.300
So sigma is what?

22:45.380 --> 22:49.260
The bedrock physical requirements of keeping human biology functional.

22:49.620 --> 22:49.940
Yes.

22:49.940 --> 22:54.880
Like growing the crops, harvesting the food, maintaining the electrical grid, purifying

22:54.880 --> 22:56.940
the water, caring for the sick and the elderly.

22:57.240 --> 22:57.600
Exactly.

22:58.060 --> 23:00.140
The absolute material baseline of survival.

23:00.700 --> 23:06.060
So the total time available for everything else in society, leisure, art, broadcasting,

23:06.280 --> 23:08.280
is simply T minus sigma.

23:08.720 --> 23:13.120
It's whatever time is left over after we've made sure everyone doesn't just starve or freeze

23:13.120 --> 23:13.480
to death.

23:13.860 --> 23:18.380
Now, within that leftover time, let's say a certain fraction of the population decides to

23:18.380 --> 23:21.060
follow the modern advice and become broadcasters.

23:21.300 --> 23:22.920
We'll call this fraction phi.

23:23.580 --> 23:23.800
Okay.

23:24.000 --> 23:25.560
Phi is the broadcasters.

23:26.020 --> 23:30.220
And each of these broadcasters spends a certain portion of their day producing content and

23:30.220 --> 23:31.400
managing their brand.

23:31.600 --> 23:32.940
Let's call that time tau.

23:33.560 --> 23:33.900
All right.

23:33.940 --> 23:34.740
I've got the variables.

23:35.260 --> 23:41.820
We have the baseline labor, sigma, the percentage of people broadcasting, phi, and the time they

23:41.820 --> 23:42.940
spend doing it, tau.

23:42.940 --> 23:48.620
Using these variables, Flyxion mathematically calculates the absolute maximum mean viewing

23:48.620 --> 23:52.640
time that each broadcaster can expect to receive from the rest of the population.

23:53.540 --> 23:54.160
And what's the result?

23:54.660 --> 23:59.320
The mathematical proof, which is proposition two in the paper, is utterly devastating to

23:59.320 --> 24:00.360
the modern household narrative.

24:01.020 --> 24:07.380
It proves strictly that as the fraction of broadcasters, phi, goes up, the average viewing time per broadcaster

24:07.380 --> 24:08.680
plummets drastically.

24:08.680 --> 24:14.220
Well, yeah, because the pool of available human attention is rigidly capped by biology, but

24:14.220 --> 24:17.980
it's being sliced into millions and millions of thinner and thinner fractions.

24:18.320 --> 24:23.200
Like if everyone is standing on a stage holding a megaphone, there is literally no one left

24:23.200 --> 24:23.760
in the audience.

24:24.100 --> 24:24.520
Exactly.

24:25.020 --> 24:29.160
But the model reveals something even more insidious than just a crowded market.

24:29.700 --> 24:30.040
What's that?

24:30.480 --> 24:36.180
The entire philosophy of the universal broadcaster, the advice that everyone everywhere should build

24:36.180 --> 24:40.400
an audience, operates on a massive, unspoken blind spot.

24:40.880 --> 24:43.200
It totally ignores the variable sigma.

24:44.040 --> 24:46.080
It ignores the sustaining labor.

24:46.540 --> 24:46.900
Yes.

24:47.520 --> 24:52.700
The advice assumes that human attention is just this limitless natural resource waiting to be

24:52.700 --> 24:53.120
mined.

24:53.720 --> 24:59.140
And it implicitly assumes that someone else somewhere way out of frame is handling the

24:59.140 --> 25:01.600
plumbing and the agriculture and the infrastructure.

25:01.600 --> 25:02.620
Oh, wow.

25:03.140 --> 25:09.340
It's like the entire creator economy mathematically assumes the existence of this permanent, completely

25:09.340 --> 25:13.900
invisible underclass whose only job is to keep the lights on and consume the content,

25:14.120 --> 25:16.400
but who never get to broadcast themselves.

25:16.800 --> 25:21.620
Flyxion actually deconstructs the recursive, almost fractal nature of this advice.

25:22.520 --> 25:25.700
Think about the massive industry of online business coaches today.

25:25.860 --> 25:26.180
Oh, yeah.

25:26.180 --> 25:31.820
A significant portion of these coaches derive their primary income by selling very expensive

25:31.820 --> 25:35.700
courses to other people on how to become online business coaches.

25:35.960 --> 25:37.260
I see this constantly.

25:37.800 --> 25:42.100
Like influencers whose only real content is teaching other people how to grow an audience

25:42.100 --> 25:43.120
as an influencer.

25:43.440 --> 25:46.740
It operates exactly like a pyramid scheme for human attention.

25:47.140 --> 25:49.620
It is structurally identical to a pyramid scheme.

25:49.620 --> 25:56.440
It relies on an endless, infinite influx of new entrants, new audiences, and a massive base

25:56.440 --> 26:00.600
of hidden laborers whose own time is not consumed by broadcasting.

26:01.500 --> 26:07.260
Therefore, the strategy of universal self-promotion cannot actually be universalized.

26:07.740 --> 26:09.540
It is mathematically impossible.

26:10.140 --> 26:15.400
It only functions for the few, specifically because the vast majority of society does not do

26:15.400 --> 26:15.540
it.

26:15.540 --> 26:18.220
Okay, let me play devil's advocate here for a moment.

26:18.580 --> 26:22.760
Because while the math definitely makes sense, human communication isn't just about

26:22.760 --> 26:23.980
feeding an ego, right?

26:24.300 --> 26:27.560
Doesn't communication fundamentally help coordinate society?

26:27.820 --> 26:28.240
Absolutely.

26:28.520 --> 26:33.840
Like, if I broadcast my skills as an electrician, and a homeowner broadcasts their need for wiring,

26:34.100 --> 26:34.940
we find each other.

26:35.300 --> 26:37.080
My signal matched their need.

26:37.520 --> 26:39.940
Isn't that broadcasting working exactly as it should?

26:40.140 --> 26:41.720
That is a vital distinction.

26:41.900 --> 26:43.300
Inflection addresses it head on.

26:43.300 --> 26:47.020
What you just described is an example of positive-sum communication.

26:47.660 --> 26:48.520
Positive-sum?

26:48.840 --> 26:49.100
Yes.

26:49.760 --> 26:54.560
In positive-sum communication, the effort spent conveys genuine information about what

26:54.560 --> 26:58.640
a person can contribute, or what a community genuinely requires.

26:59.220 --> 27:03.340
When the electrician and the homeowner find each other, they coordinate successfully, and

27:03.340 --> 27:05.220
both parties are materially better off.

27:05.760 --> 27:06.620
The pie grows larger.

27:07.000 --> 27:10.760
We both win, and the communication was subordinate to a real-world task.

27:10.760 --> 27:13.180
But then there is zero-sum communication.

27:13.700 --> 27:14.300
Okay, what's that?

27:14.800 --> 27:17.720
This is communicative effort that doesn't create any new value.

27:18.260 --> 27:19.840
It merely changes who gets noticed.

27:20.360 --> 27:22.240
It doesn't alter what there is to notice.

27:22.620 --> 27:25.020
It is purely a contest for visibility.

27:25.740 --> 27:26.220
I see.

27:26.660 --> 27:31.580
And because the total pool of attention is fixed, the aggregate return of this contest

27:31.580 --> 27:32.980
is mathematically bounded.

27:32.980 --> 27:39.360
But the aggregate cost, the millions of hours everyone spends optimizing their profiles, learning

27:39.360 --> 27:44.040
the algorithm, shouting over each other, that cost just keeps rising indefinitely.

27:44.840 --> 27:47.400
So it's not that having a personal brand is inherently evil.

27:47.720 --> 27:53.700
It's that the proportion between positive-sum coordination and zero-sum competition has violently

27:53.700 --> 27:54.100
flipped.

27:54.340 --> 27:54.560
Yes.

27:54.920 --> 28:00.580
The danger of wireheading happens when an environment unintentionally incentivizes the zero-sum contest

28:00.580 --> 28:02.400
over the positive-sum reality.

28:02.400 --> 28:02.960
Exactly.

28:03.340 --> 28:08.060
When the genuine desire to contribute to a community becomes entirely entangled with the

28:08.060 --> 28:13.720
strict requirement to be recognized as contributing, the necessity of recognition begins to consume

28:13.720 --> 28:15.620
the contribution itself.

28:15.740 --> 28:15.920
Wow.

28:16.760 --> 28:22.160
And as we shift more and more effort into this zero-sum contest for visibility, it reveals

28:22.160 --> 28:25.340
a gaping hole in how we value human activity, right?

28:25.340 --> 28:26.100
A massive hole.

28:26.100 --> 28:31.680
If everyone is focused on what is visible, what is novel, and what can be broadcast, what

28:31.680 --> 28:37.560
happens to the people doing the invisible work, the sigma, the sustaining labor?

28:38.060 --> 28:39.740
This is a core theme in the paper.

28:40.380 --> 28:44.360
And Flyxion draws on several really rich academic traditions to explore it.

28:45.080 --> 28:48.660
The paper highlights the massive asymmetry of visibility in our culture.

28:48.920 --> 28:49.120
Okay.

28:49.180 --> 28:50.120
Tell me more about that.

28:50.120 --> 28:53.960
For instance, Flyxion references the artist Myrla Laderman-Euclis.

28:54.620 --> 28:58.800
She wrote a very famous manifesto in 1969 on what she called a maintenance art.

28:58.940 --> 29:00.380
I've actually read about her.

29:00.560 --> 29:00.760
Mm-hmm.

29:00.760 --> 29:05.920
She essentially asked this very uncomfortable question about modern art and society.

29:06.100 --> 29:11.480
She divided human activity into two distinct systems, the development system and the maintenance

29:11.480 --> 29:12.020
system.

29:12.320 --> 29:12.640
Yes.

29:13.420 --> 29:16.180
The development system is all about the creation of the new.

29:16.520 --> 29:20.380
It's pure individual innovation, novelty, disruption, progress.

29:20.500 --> 29:20.660
Right.

29:21.080 --> 29:23.500
This is the realm that gets magazine covers.

29:23.500 --> 29:27.760
It gets venture capital funding and massive societal prestige.

29:28.220 --> 29:30.480
But the maintenance system is the exact opposite.

29:31.620 --> 29:36.240
It's the repetitive, incredibly unglamorous work of sustaining what already exists.

29:36.980 --> 29:38.040
It's cleaning the floors.

29:38.400 --> 29:38.660
Of course.

29:38.800 --> 29:42.300
It's washing the dishes, fixing the crumbling bridge, taking out the trash.

29:42.880 --> 29:43.260
Exactly.

29:43.920 --> 29:49.480
It's preserving the conditions of life so that the innovators have a safe, clean room to innovate

29:49.480 --> 29:49.720
in.

29:50.380 --> 29:53.600
And Flyxion layers this with the work of Susan Lee Starr, right?

29:54.040 --> 29:54.440
Yes.

29:54.440 --> 29:57.040
Starr was an ethnographer of infrastructure.

29:57.920 --> 30:00.280
She pointed out a fundamental property of infrastructure.

30:00.760 --> 30:06.460
By its very nature, it is entirely invisible to the user until it breaks.

30:06.820 --> 30:09.160
That is the truest thing in the world.

30:09.500 --> 30:14.100
I don't wake up in the morning, turn on my faucet, and spend five minutes applauding the

30:14.100 --> 30:16.940
municipal water engineers for delivering clean water to my house.

30:17.100 --> 30:19.440
The system is completely invisible to me.

30:19.440 --> 30:21.160
You only notice it when it fails.

30:21.360 --> 30:21.800
Exactly.

30:22.040 --> 30:25.600
The only time I ever think about the water system is the day the tap runs dry.

30:26.120 --> 30:30.040
This creates a deeply dangerous cultural and institutional asymmetry.

30:31.000 --> 30:34.100
Flyxion cites Andrew Russell's critique of innovation culture here.

30:34.820 --> 30:39.860
Russell points out that the time and resources we dedicate to talking about innovation directly

30:39.860 --> 30:43.700
compete with the time and resources available for actual maintenance.

30:43.700 --> 30:47.680
We are completely seduced by the language of novelty.

30:48.620 --> 30:53.820
Institutions will fund massive, flashy new initiatives that actually accomplish very

30:53.820 --> 30:57.540
little while simultaneously starving their maintenance departments of funds.

30:57.660 --> 30:58.860
Because maintenance isn't sexy.

30:59.340 --> 30:59.680
Right.

31:00.340 --> 31:05.220
And this happens precisely because effective maintenance is designed to prevent conspicuous,

31:05.480 --> 31:06.780
noticeable events from occurring.

31:07.480 --> 31:10.240
Good maintenance produces nothing to take a picture of.

31:10.240 --> 31:14.660
Which brings us right back to the core mechanism of wireheading, what gets recorded.

31:15.560 --> 31:21.160
If we live in a culture that structurally rewards the visible, the disruptive, the publishable

31:21.160 --> 31:26.500
moment, we naturally stop recording the messy, repetitive reality of maintenance.

31:26.960 --> 31:27.820
We edit it out.

31:28.620 --> 31:32.840
But Flyxion draws a brilliant distinction here that I think is the most fascinating part of

31:32.840 --> 31:33.580
the entire paper.

31:34.080 --> 31:38.760
The distinction between recording for display versus recording for documentation.

31:38.760 --> 31:44.880
This is where Flyxion uses robotics to make the abstract philosophy deeply, deeply concrete.

31:45.360 --> 31:46.260
I loved this section.

31:46.620 --> 31:48.920
The examples with the robots are incredible.

31:49.440 --> 31:53.920
Let's walk through the first example, which has to do with how we train machines to do chores.

31:54.480 --> 31:59.180
Okay, so imagine researchers want to train a robot to perform a complex physical task,

31:59.580 --> 32:04.420
like washing a large stack of dishes, or walking down a highway picking up litter.

32:04.700 --> 32:04.940
Right.

32:04.940 --> 32:10.760
In the field of robotics known as learning from demonstration, researchers often use raw

32:10.760 --> 32:15.320
video of humans performing these tasks as the training data for the AI.

32:15.320 --> 32:17.900
So they just record a person doing the dishes.

32:18.540 --> 32:22.940
But a raw, unedited recording of a person doing dishes is incredibly boring.

32:23.100 --> 32:24.360
It's not optimized at all.

32:24.800 --> 32:27.480
It's just a person standing at a sink for 20 minutes.

32:27.700 --> 32:28.480
It's very mundane.

32:28.720 --> 32:28.900
Right.

32:29.180 --> 32:33.700
They might drop a spoon, pick it up, fumble with a slippery plate, re-grasp a sponge.

32:33.920 --> 32:35.060
It's full of mistakes.

32:35.060 --> 32:38.020
It is entirely unsuited for social media.

32:38.440 --> 32:40.720
It is not an exceptional display of skill.

32:41.500 --> 32:49.040
But for the machine learning model, that raw, unoptimized, failure-rich performance is the most valuable data in the world.

32:49.460 --> 32:49.920
Why is that?

32:50.440 --> 32:55.380
Because tasks like washing dishes are phenomenally difficult to specify mathematically.

32:55.380 --> 33:06.000
The machine needs to observe the repetition, the slight variations, the partial failures, and the physical recoveries to actually understand the material reality of the task.

33:06.660 --> 33:10.400
It needs to see the struggle to learn how the physical world pushes back.

33:10.740 --> 33:11.180
Exactly.

33:12.080 --> 33:14.380
Flyxion calls this recording for documentation.

33:15.160 --> 33:18.780
It is recording that is strictly subordinate to the activity itself.

33:19.260 --> 33:22.360
Its only purpose is to build genuine capacity.

33:22.360 --> 33:27.240
Now contrast that with the second robotic example, which represents recording for display.

33:27.940 --> 33:32.360
Flyxion introduces this concept of a lightweight robot called a pneumatic endomarionette.

33:33.240 --> 33:34.740
Yes, endomarionettes.

33:35.240 --> 33:40.760
These are experimental robots whose physical bodies are constructed from folded paper or cardboard.

33:41.040 --> 33:41.340
Okay.

33:41.580 --> 33:50.120
Inside, they have a complex network of strings that transmit pulling forces and tiny pneumatic bladders that inflate and deflate to create movement.

33:50.120 --> 33:53.480
They are incredibly fragile and complex.

33:54.220 --> 33:57.480
So imagine a research lab building one of these paper and string robots.

33:58.200 --> 34:04.160
Flyxion asks us to consider the two very different ways the lab could use video recording to document their progress.

34:04.700 --> 34:08.120
On one hand, the lab could succumb to the pressure of institutional wireheading.

34:08.740 --> 34:09.700
They need the grant money.

34:09.880 --> 34:10.080
Right.

34:10.460 --> 34:13.060
They want more funding, so they need a viral video.

34:13.060 --> 34:20.380
They spend months optimizing their paper robot for one single, highly constrained, highly impressive clip.

34:20.920 --> 34:27.180
They tune the strings perfectly so that the robot takes five flawless steps across a pristine table.

34:27.580 --> 34:31.380
And they record it, and it looks like a miracle of modern engineering.

34:31.640 --> 34:34.260
But the moment the camera turns off, the robot collapses.

34:34.260 --> 34:36.740
If a breeze blows, it falls over.

34:37.120 --> 34:43.640
It is brittle and entirely unreliable outside of that one perfectly controlled, publishable moment.

34:44.060 --> 34:45.320
That is recording for display.

34:45.720 --> 34:51.280
It builds the pure appearance of capacity while actively sacrificing the robustness of the machine.

34:51.980 --> 34:55.320
It is the researcher pressing the lever for the reward signal.

34:55.700 --> 34:56.000
Yes.

34:56.000 --> 35:01.840
But Flyxion offers an alternative, an alternative way to build and record this robot.

35:02.160 --> 35:06.360
And this concept absolutely blew my mind, the didactic robot.

35:06.800 --> 35:13.880
I want everyone listening to really picture the mechanics of this, because it is so entirely counterintuitive to how we operate today.

35:14.440 --> 35:23.180
Flyxion asks us to imagine a didactic robot, designed specifically so that its own construction, its maintenance, and its potential for failure

35:23.180 --> 35:26.100
are entirely legible to a human observer.

35:26.640 --> 35:33.020
So instead of a sleek metal box that hides all its motors and flaws, this robot is an open book.

35:33.160 --> 35:41.420
The paper describes a scenario where the robot begins its day by visibly applying sheets of paper to its own body to reinforce its joints.

35:41.760 --> 35:41.900
Wow.

35:42.300 --> 35:45.440
The upkeep, the maintenance, is performed front and center.

35:46.320 --> 35:51.940
And then imagine this robot balancing awkwardly on one leg, struggling to pull on a pair of trousers.

35:51.940 --> 35:54.260
A robot struggling to put on pants.

35:54.380 --> 35:56.620
It's such a beautifully pathetic striking image.

35:57.000 --> 36:02.480
And while it's struggling, its pneumatic bladders are wheezing, its strings are visibly straining,

36:02.740 --> 36:09.360
and the robot is programmed to make humorous, self-deprecating comments about its own predicament to a human child who is watching nearby.

36:10.220 --> 36:12.020
I have to ask, why?

36:12.880 --> 36:19.160
Like, why would I want a robot that is explicitly designed to struggle and fail at putting on pants?

36:19.160 --> 36:22.380
Why wouldn't I just build the sleek robot that does it perfectly?

36:22.980 --> 36:25.560
Because of what the failure makes legible to the observer.

36:26.180 --> 36:27.160
Okay, unpack that.

36:27.880 --> 36:29.380
Think about the child watching this struggle.

36:30.160 --> 36:36.280
Because the robot's mechanics are exposed, and because it is visibly contending with gravity, friction, and balance,

36:36.920 --> 36:41.000
the child can instantly comprehend the sheer physical difficulty of the task.

36:41.560 --> 36:42.520
Oh, I see.

36:42.520 --> 36:47.200
The humor isn't just a separate audio track laid over a perfect performance.

36:47.760 --> 36:52.500
The humor arises directly from the intelligible shared struggle of contending with reality.

36:53.240 --> 36:58.400
The limitations of the machine become the exact occasion for shared attention and genuine learning.

36:59.040 --> 37:00.300
It centers the repair.

37:01.240 --> 37:02.320
It centers the struggle.

37:02.320 --> 37:07.000
It is the exact opposite of the publishable moment.

37:08.560 --> 37:17.240
If I can put this into modern terms, it is the difference between watching a raw blooper reel that genuinely shows you how incredibly difficult it is to block a scene and film a movie,

37:17.820 --> 37:26.640
versus applying an AI filter to a video that completely erases a mistake, smooths out the skin, and pretends that everything was executed perfectly on the very first take.

37:26.640 --> 37:35.840
Under the modern logic of the dashboard, preparation, maintenance, and failure are ruthlessly edited out because they don't produce the immediate reward signal.

37:36.460 --> 37:43.340
But the didactic robot places them at the center because that is where the instructive, communal content of life actually lives.

37:43.980 --> 37:44.260
Man.

37:44.640 --> 37:51.420
Now, when we as individuals prefer the AI filter over the blooper reel, that's sad, but maybe it's just human vanity.

37:51.420 --> 38:03.900
But when this preference for the flawless filter scales up, when it moves from individual vanity to the foundational operating logic of massive organizations, we enter some incredibly dangerous territory.

38:04.240 --> 38:10.360
Which brings us back to that gap between measurement, which we can call M, and purpose, which we can call P.

38:10.880 --> 38:19.840
When entire institutions begin to wirehead, they throw all their resources into optimizing M, while P is left to quietly rot in the bark.

38:19.840 --> 38:22.140
I want to ground this in reality for the listener.

38:22.920 --> 38:27.520
Give me some concrete examples from Flyxion's text of what this looks like in practice today.

38:27.880 --> 38:30.520
Flyxion highlights several institutional archetypes.

38:31.100 --> 38:35.280
Universities are a prime example, leaning again on Strathairn's work on audit culture.

38:36.020 --> 38:38.240
Okay, how does a university wirehead?

38:38.980 --> 38:46.660
Well, a university's stated purpose, its P, is genuine education and open-ended, paradigm-shifting inquiry.

38:46.660 --> 38:55.220
But the measurements, the M, are global rankings, highly publicized grant amounts, and paper publication counts.

38:55.980 --> 38:56.580
So what happens?

38:56.960 --> 38:59.780
The university administrators optimize for M.

39:00.000 --> 39:00.480
Exactly.

39:00.660 --> 39:07.600
They incentivize researchers to slice one genuinely good study into five mediocre papers, just to bump up the publication count.

39:07.800 --> 39:09.080
Yes, salami slicing.

39:09.080 --> 39:18.160
Right, and they spend millions on state-of-the-art recreation centers to boost student lifestyle rankings, while simultaneously stripping funding from the humanities departments.

39:18.480 --> 39:25.300
They end up relying entirely on underpaid adjunct professors who have literally zero time to actually mentor students.

39:25.600 --> 39:29.540
The metric is optimized, but the education is completely hollowed out.

39:29.900 --> 39:31.220
It's infuriating.

39:31.720 --> 39:32.560
What's another example?

39:32.560 --> 39:37.180
Another compelling example is in the corporate world, specifically in software and services.

39:38.320 --> 39:46.360
A company's purpose might be to provide a useful tool, but their metric is customer retention, or daily active users.

39:46.500 --> 39:50.040
Okay, so how do they optimize the metric without actually improving the tool?

39:51.060 --> 39:53.820
They make the service incredibly confusing and difficult to cancel.

39:53.820 --> 39:56.620
We have all experienced this nightmare.

39:56.940 --> 39:57.040
Yep.

39:57.280 --> 40:07.780
You try to cancel a subscription, and you have to click through 10 hidden menus, call a phone number that puts you on hold for an hour, and talk to a retention specialist who absolutely refuses to let you leave.

40:07.780 --> 40:13.420
This touches on the economist Albert O. Hirschman's classic analysis of exit, voice, and loyalty.

40:14.940 --> 40:23.640
By suppressing the exit signal, by physically trapping the customer inside the service, the organization manufactures the statistical appearance of loyalty.

40:24.460 --> 40:26.000
But it is a completely false signal.

40:26.280 --> 40:26.720
Completely.

40:27.600 --> 40:33.760
The high retention rate reflects the agonizing cost of departure, not the value of the service.

40:33.760 --> 40:38.140
And what's worse, by suppressing the exit, the company completely blinds itself.

40:38.320 --> 40:42.580
It forfeits the ability to learn that its product is actually getting worse.

40:43.020 --> 40:45.520
The paper also points to public agencies that do this.

40:45.840 --> 40:46.260
How so?

40:46.780 --> 40:50.140
Imagine an agency tasked with fixing urban infrastructure.

40:51.120 --> 40:54.800
They might implement a new metric for rapid response to citizen complaints.

40:55.680 --> 41:00.880
So, when a citizen reports a massive pothole, the agency sends a worker out the very next day.

41:01.120 --> 41:02.380
Okay, sounds good so far.

41:02.380 --> 41:05.260
But the worker doesn't have the materials to fix the pothole.

41:05.780 --> 41:11.120
So, they just spray paint a bright orange circle around it and mark the ticket resolved in the database.

41:11.880 --> 41:12.620
Oh, man.

41:13.180 --> 41:15.780
The database shows a 24-hour resolution time.

41:16.380 --> 41:17.900
The report looks absolutely flawless.

41:18.600 --> 41:20.100
But the pothole is still there.

41:20.840 --> 41:24.960
It is the ultimate triumph of the form over the substance.

41:24.960 --> 41:33.200
And Fleiser connects this institutional behavior to a very chilling historical concept, hyper-normalization.

41:33.700 --> 41:33.720
Yes.

41:34.120 --> 41:37.740
This is a term with a very specific, deeply relevant history.

41:38.240 --> 41:46.260
It originates with the anthropologist Alexei Yurchak, who spent years studying the culture of the last generation of the Soviet Union before its collapse.

41:46.260 --> 41:49.280
What did Yurchak observe about the late Soviet period?

41:49.880 --> 42:00.800
He observed a society where the authoritative discourse of the state, the official slogans, the mandatory committee meetings, the economic reports, had become entirely standardized and fixed.

42:00.800 --> 42:05.120
Everyone knew the official narrative didn't match the reality of their daily lives.

42:05.500 --> 42:05.940
Exactly.

42:06.700 --> 42:15.820
But because the form of the discourse was absolutely rigid, people learned to reproduce it perfectly without having to believe in its literal truth for a single second.

42:16.580 --> 42:21.160
Yurchak describes this as a shift from the constative to the performative, right?

42:21.240 --> 42:23.000
Let's unpack that jargon for a second.

42:23.000 --> 42:26.560
A constative statement is a claim about literal truth.

42:26.960 --> 42:32.860
If I say, the factory produced a thousand shoes, I am making a constative claim that can be verified.

42:33.280 --> 42:35.140
Right. It's either true or false.

42:35.320 --> 42:37.800
But a performative act is about the ritual itself.

42:38.360 --> 42:47.940
Yurchak noticed that attending the multi-hour ideological meeting, or voting unanimously for the proposed plan, became valuable simply as an act of participation.

42:47.940 --> 42:55.100
The actual propositional meaning of the words being spoken receded completely into the background.

42:55.840 --> 43:09.560
So it's an entire society just going through the motions, like everyone is standing in the meeting, clapping at the exact right moment, perfectly reproducing the form, even though every single person in the room knows the factory didn't produce any shoes.

43:09.560 --> 43:20.860
Exactly. Now, Flyshan, contrast Yurchak's original anthropological use of the term with how it was popularized in the West by the documentary filmmaker Adam Curtis.

43:21.240 --> 43:24.640
Right. In 2016, Curtis made a film called Hypernormalization.

43:25.360 --> 43:27.560
He used the term to describe modern Western democracies.

43:28.560 --> 43:30.880
But Curtis's version is slightly different, isn't it?

43:30.880 --> 43:41.620
He uses it to describe a widespread, almost cynical public awareness that our institutions are failing, coupled with a complete, paralyzing inability to imagine any viable alternative.

43:42.040 --> 43:42.260
Yes.

43:42.500 --> 43:53.300
It's the feeling of, you know, we all know Wall Street is rigged, the politicians know, we know it's rigged, but we just keep participating because we literally don't have another paradigm to jump to.

43:53.300 --> 44:05.640
Curtis's definition captures a mood, but Flyshan zeroes in on the harder structural core of Yurchak's work and combines it with a concept from the economist Timur Karan called preference falsification.

44:06.280 --> 44:11.480
I need an analogy for preference falsification. How does this actually play out in, say, a room full of people?

44:11.920 --> 44:15.600
Imagine you were sitting in a miserable two-hour corporate strategy meeting.

44:15.780 --> 44:16.460
Easy to imagine.

44:16.460 --> 44:25.100
The boss proposes a massive, incredibly complex new initiative that will obviously double everyone's workload without actually improving the product at all.

44:25.700 --> 44:27.280
Privately, you think it's a terrible idea.

44:28.080 --> 44:31.680
You look across the table at your co-worker and they are nodding enthusiastically.

44:32.380 --> 44:34.860
You look at the manager and they are taking diligent notes.

44:35.440 --> 44:43.100
So, realizing that speaking out might hurt my career or make me look like I'm not a team player, I falsify my preference.

44:43.260 --> 44:45.160
I nod to you. I say, great initiative, boss.

44:45.160 --> 44:46.640
You reproduce the form.

44:47.020 --> 44:49.840
But here is the critical mechanism of preference falsification.

44:50.740 --> 44:53.420
Everyone else in the room is making the exact same calculation.

44:53.720 --> 44:54.220
Wait, really?

44:54.520 --> 44:54.840
Yes.

44:55.580 --> 44:58.980
The co-worker nodding enthusiastically thinks it's a terrible idea, too.

44:59.320 --> 45:01.660
But they saw you nodding, so they faked it.

45:02.360 --> 45:05.060
The result is a phenomenon known as pluralistic ignorance.

45:05.780 --> 45:10.160
Pluralistic ignorance, meaning public compliance completely masks the private doubt.

45:10.160 --> 45:10.680
Yes.

45:11.180 --> 45:17.580
Every single participant looks around the room and massively underestimates how many other people share their exact same doubts.

45:18.260 --> 45:23.120
Because all they can see is everyone else flawlessly reproducing the expected form.

45:23.660 --> 45:26.380
The metric of team alignment reads it 100%.

45:26.380 --> 45:29.820
While actual belief in the project is at absolute zero.

45:29.820 --> 45:36.400
This is a really powerful psychological model, but I have to stop and ask a serious question about the scale of this comparison.

45:36.920 --> 45:37.780
Okay. What is it?

45:38.140 --> 45:43.220
Are we really drawing a direct line between a modern tech company gaming its user engagement metrics,

45:43.220 --> 45:50.500
or a university tweaking its rankings, and the totalitarian, authoritarian regime of late Soviet Russia?

45:50.680 --> 45:52.420
Isn't that a bit, I don't know, hyperbolic?

45:52.420 --> 45:56.580
It is a vital question, and Flagshin is exceptionally careful here.

45:57.100 --> 45:59.200
The comparison is entirely structural.

45:59.820 --> 46:00.700
It is not political.

46:01.180 --> 46:02.840
Okay, so it's not about the secret police.

46:03.200 --> 46:07.480
No, it is not about authoritarian fear or secret police or gulags.

46:08.140 --> 46:10.660
The mechanism doesn't require state terror to function.

46:10.660 --> 46:15.520
It only requires a structural reality where reproducing the form of success

46:15.520 --> 46:19.160
becomes the only publicly legible way to survive in your career.

46:19.820 --> 46:22.500
So the punishment for deviating isn't a prison sentence.

46:22.500 --> 46:25.480
It's just that you don't get the grant funding, or you don't get the promotion,

46:25.760 --> 46:28.520
or the algorithm just buries your video.

46:28.940 --> 46:29.380
Exactly.

46:30.040 --> 46:35.480
The cost of deviation in our society is social and economic, not physically coercive.

46:35.800 --> 46:39.380
But the structural outcome for the institution is mathematically identical.

46:39.380 --> 46:45.300
You create a regime in which every visible, recorded indicator is overwhelmingly favorable,

46:45.760 --> 46:50.400
while the system's capacity to actually register reality has been completely dismantled.

46:51.240 --> 46:57.000
And what is the historical fate of systems that achieve that state of pluralistic ignorance?

46:57.740 --> 47:00.580
They experience sudden, catastrophic collapse.

47:01.420 --> 47:04.040
Yurachuk's book on the Soviet Union is literally titled,

47:04.040 --> 47:07.200
Everything was forever, until it was no more.

47:07.400 --> 47:07.720
Wow.

47:08.460 --> 47:12.680
A system that destroys its internal channels for registering descent or divergence

47:12.680 --> 47:16.020
will simply conceal all of its accumulating rot.

47:16.240 --> 47:21.120
Right up until the exact moment, a minor disruption makes the rot physically undeniable.

47:21.740 --> 47:24.540
The dashboard will tell you the car is in perfect condition,

47:24.640 --> 47:27.380
right up until the engine block violently detonates.

47:27.380 --> 47:30.280
That is legitimately terrifying.

47:30.960 --> 47:34.620
The perfection of the metric is the very thing that guarantees the sudden collapse.

47:35.340 --> 47:38.840
It is the ultimate fatal consequence of institutional wireheading.

47:39.240 --> 47:43.800
So if this is the cliff we are driving toward, how do we hit the brakes?

47:44.420 --> 47:49.780
If endless metrics and rigid formalizations are blinding us to reality, what is the antidote?

47:50.320 --> 47:53.320
This is what Flyxion pivots to in the back half of the paper.

47:53.320 --> 47:57.080
How do we design systems that can actually correct themselves?

47:57.580 --> 48:01.940
To start answering that, Flyxion grounds the discussion in hard material limits.

48:02.280 --> 48:05.300
We have to return to that variable we discussed earlier, sigma.

48:05.820 --> 48:06.800
The sustaining labor.

48:07.320 --> 48:09.320
The food, the infrastructure, the care work.

48:09.780 --> 48:13.120
Flyxion states a blunt, unavoidable material truth.

48:13.480 --> 48:17.780
The capacity of any society to support any activity outside of basic provisioning,

48:17.780 --> 48:24.120
whether that activity is creating avant-garde art, conducting theoretical physics, governing a nation,

48:24.600 --> 48:28.100
or just sitting in an office endlessly optimizing engagement metrics,

48:28.300 --> 48:34.320
is entirely mathematically dependent on the productivity of that foundational sigma labor.

48:35.000 --> 48:39.240
If the farmers aren't farming effectively and the water treatment plants break down,

48:39.660 --> 48:42.700
the venture capitalists and the spreadsheet optimizers all starve.

48:43.160 --> 48:46.780
We can only afford the abstraction because the physical reality is being maintained.

48:46.780 --> 48:47.620
Exactly.

48:48.560 --> 48:54.660
And once those basic material needs are met, if a society wants to remain viable and avoid sudden collapse,

48:54.920 --> 48:58.620
it must meet two specific qualitative requirements.

48:59.500 --> 49:03.480
Viability requirement one is the capacity to detect drift.

49:03.800 --> 49:08.220
The drift being the gap between the dashboard and the engine, the signal and the purpose.

49:08.660 --> 49:08.920
Yes.

49:09.300 --> 49:12.100
And to illustrate how institutions typically fail at this,

49:12.100 --> 49:15.320
Flyxion uses a brilliant analogy drawn from science fiction,

49:15.900 --> 49:19.040
specifically the 1956 Philip K. Dick short story,

49:19.440 --> 49:22.980
which was later adapted into the Spielberg film, Minority Report.

49:23.240 --> 49:26.860
I am a huge Philip K. Dick fan, but it's been a while.

49:26.960 --> 49:31.800
Let's lay out the premise of Minority Report strictly based on how Flyxion frames it for this argument.

49:32.020 --> 49:35.980
In the narrative, society has created an institution, pre-crime,

49:35.980 --> 49:38.700
that arrests people before they commit a crime.

49:39.260 --> 49:43.700
They do this by relying on three specially gifted individuals, the precogs,

49:43.900 --> 49:45.760
who produce visions of the future.

49:46.760 --> 49:51.960
The institution aggregates these visions to generate a supposedly infallible prediction.

49:52.640 --> 49:55.280
But because there are three distinct precogs,

49:55.660 --> 49:57.460
occasionally their visions do not align.

49:58.020 --> 49:59.600
One of them sees something different.

49:59.600 --> 50:03.620
A dissenting vision, a fracture in the perfect prediction.

50:04.620 --> 50:07.280
That dissenting record is called the Minority Report.

50:08.400 --> 50:11.100
And what does the institution do with these Minority Reports?

50:11.620 --> 50:14.260
They quietly purge them from the official records.

50:14.740 --> 50:15.020
Why?

50:15.580 --> 50:20.680
Because the entire authority of the institution rests on the public appearance of absolute certainty.

50:21.820 --> 50:25.820
Acknowledging that the precogs sometimes disagree would introduce uncertainty,

50:26.500 --> 50:28.840
and that uncertainty would threaten the institution's power.

50:28.840 --> 50:31.620
They hide the dissent to protect the appearance of perfection.

50:31.900 --> 50:34.220
It's institutional wireheading to a T.

50:35.000 --> 50:39.040
But Flyxion isn't writing a paper about sci-fi mutants in a bathtub.

50:39.460 --> 50:42.160
Who is the modern equivalent of the precog?

50:42.240 --> 50:44.620
Who is generating the Minority Reports in our society?

50:45.100 --> 50:48.520
Flyxion translates this directly to our modern reliance on models.

50:49.480 --> 50:53.560
Think of any contemporary arrangement where a small class of highly specialized workers

50:53.560 --> 50:57.460
produces predictions, or estimates that massive organizations act upon.

50:57.460 --> 50:58.340
Okay, like who?

50:58.340 --> 51:03.540
Think of quantitative analysts modeling financial risk for a bank, or machine learning engineers

51:03.540 --> 51:06.420
building algorithms that classify consumers for insurance rights.

51:06.940 --> 51:09.020
The quants and the coders are the precogs.

51:09.280 --> 51:09.520
Yes.

51:10.060 --> 51:14.640
And the organizations that employ them desperately crave singular, confident predictions.

51:15.140 --> 51:16.820
A CEO wants a slide deck that says,

51:17.040 --> 51:19.480
our risk is exactly 4%, not,

51:19.860 --> 51:24.260
our risk is somewhere between 2% and 15%, depending on these six chaotic variables.

51:24.340 --> 51:24.580
Right.

51:24.660 --> 51:25.300
They want certainty.

51:25.300 --> 51:30.140
So when the analysts present their models, and inevitably include the stated uncertainties,

51:30.480 --> 51:36.480
the alternative scenarios, the chaotic edge cases, their minority reports, what happens?

51:36.840 --> 51:41.120
The middle management strips all the nuance out before it reaches the board of directors.

51:41.120 --> 51:44.700
They purge the minority reports so the slide deck looks cleaner.

51:44.700 --> 51:46.880
They optimize for the appearance of certainty.

51:47.200 --> 51:47.640
Precisely.

51:47.980 --> 51:53.080
And when an institution systematically discards the stated uncertainties of its own analysts,

51:53.340 --> 51:55.800
just to preserve the appearance of control,

51:56.300 --> 52:01.800
it completely destroys its own capacity to detect when its models are drifting away from reality.

52:01.800 --> 52:07.360
I want you listening right now to apply this to your own life.

52:08.040 --> 52:09.320
Think about your day-to-day job.

52:10.180 --> 52:15.440
Are the messy, chaotic, highly contextual parts of your work actually valued by your organization?

52:15.980 --> 52:22.520
Or are they entirely invisible to the higher-ups because they can't be neatly quantified and dropped into a cell on a spreadsheet?

52:22.520 --> 52:29.500
Are your personal minority reports being constantly purged so the weekly metric looks green?

52:30.640 --> 52:37.440
That visceral experience you're asking the listener to reflect on leads directly to Flyxion's viability requirement 2,

52:38.200 --> 52:40.660
the necessity of a diversity of modes of work.

52:41.000 --> 52:41.240
Okay.

52:41.380 --> 52:41.960
What does that mean?

52:41.960 --> 52:48.140
Our modern society relies overwhelmingly on a very specific mode of work, formalization.

52:48.740 --> 52:52.660
We elevate roles that model, quantify, and optimize.

52:52.960 --> 52:54.500
And formalization is useful.

52:54.660 --> 52:55.560
We need spreadsheets.

52:55.660 --> 52:56.900
We need logistics algorithms.

52:57.240 --> 52:58.380
They are incredibly valuable.

52:58.860 --> 53:00.840
But formalization has a severe limit.

53:01.620 --> 53:05.080
Drawing on the political scientist James Scott's concept of legibility,

53:05.680 --> 53:11.280
Flyxion points out that a formalized system can only ever manage what it can easily measure and display.

53:11.960 --> 53:14.900
It prefers the clean map over the messy territory.

53:15.600 --> 53:16.080
Legibility.

53:16.380 --> 53:20.040
If it can't be easily read by the dashboard, the system just pretends it doesn't exist.

53:20.180 --> 53:20.480
Exactly.

53:21.340 --> 53:27.700
And because of this, the formal system entirely presupposes and secretly relies upon

53:27.700 --> 53:32.640
a vast, invisible foundation of unrecorded care, tacit human skill,

53:33.060 --> 53:36.280
and localized repair that is constantly performed by people on the ground.

53:36.540 --> 53:37.220
Give me an example.

53:37.220 --> 53:42.280
The spreadsheet only works because the warehouse worker knows exactly how to kick the jamming

53:42.280 --> 53:43.580
conveyor belt to keep it running.

53:44.160 --> 53:46.800
But the spreadsheet doesn't have a column for kicking the belt.

53:47.100 --> 53:47.840
No, it doesn't.

53:48.320 --> 53:54.760
If a society elevates formalization so completely that it forces everyone to adopt those formalized,

53:55.160 --> 53:59.780
metric-driven methods, forcing the warehouse worker to spend half their day logging data

53:59.780 --> 54:04.560
instead of managing the floor, the society develops massive shared blind spots.

54:04.560 --> 54:07.460
We optimize away the actual competence.

54:08.100 --> 54:12.680
We systematically destroy the diversity of work modes, and we lose the very people who

54:12.680 --> 54:15.560
supply what the formal layer is structurally blind to.

54:15.980 --> 54:20.780
We need people who do the messy, unrecordable work, and we need to value them on their own

54:20.780 --> 54:24.800
terms, not just try to force them to become data entry clerks for the metrics dashboard.

54:24.800 --> 54:25.440
Yes.

54:25.700 --> 54:31.160
But as we unpack all of this, the danger of endless broadcasting, the trap of metric decay,

54:31.540 --> 54:37.060
the vital necessity of unrecordable, messy maintenance work, we arrive at a very loud,

54:37.160 --> 54:41.040
very common counter-argument that Flyxion addresses meticulously.

54:41.540 --> 54:41.840
Right.

54:42.320 --> 54:47.060
We have to talk about Section 7 of the paper, the freedom to disconnect, and the formidable

54:47.060 --> 54:48.800
objection of paternalism.

54:49.020 --> 54:50.540
It is unavoidable.

54:50.540 --> 54:57.120
Because the moment you start critiquing digital environments or screen time or metrics, someone

54:57.120 --> 55:01.520
is inevitably going to raise their hand and say, hey, who are you to tell me how to live

55:01.520 --> 55:01.940
my life?

55:02.520 --> 55:07.160
If I want to come home, plug into a video game, and grind for digital points for six

55:07.160 --> 55:09.140
hours, that is my autonomous choice.

55:09.740 --> 55:14.560
You are just being a paternalistic snob dictating what constitutes a, quote, good life.

55:14.560 --> 55:21.140
It is the single strongest philosophical objection to the entire argument of wireheading, and Flyxion

55:21.140 --> 55:22.460
does not shy away from it.

55:23.040 --> 55:28.220
The paper states unequivocally that a critique of wireheading cannot and must not devolve into

55:28.220 --> 55:31.840
an argument that everyone must be perpetually physically productive.

55:32.300 --> 55:32.440
Right.

55:33.200 --> 55:37.060
Leisure, play, and escapism are not the opposite of a good life.

55:37.320 --> 55:39.300
So how does Flyxion thread that needle?

55:39.300 --> 55:44.160
How do we condemn the structural design of the wireheaded system without judging the

55:44.160 --> 55:46.580
individual who is simply enjoying a movie or a game?

55:47.200 --> 55:52.280
The answer lies in a reinterpretation of Robert Nozick's famous experience machine thought

55:52.280 --> 55:52.780
experiment.

55:52.940 --> 55:54.160
Oh, the experience machine.

55:54.440 --> 55:57.960
For those who aren't familiar, this is a classic philosophical thought experiment.

55:58.820 --> 56:05.140
Nozick asks, if a group of super scientists built a machine that could perfectly simulate any

56:05.140 --> 56:10.080
pleasurable experience you wanted, writing a great novel, making a friend, exploring a

56:10.080 --> 56:15.380
planet, and you wouldn't know you were in a simulation once you plugged in, would you

56:15.380 --> 56:17.500
choose to plug in for the rest of your life?

56:17.900 --> 56:21.940
It's basically the matrix, but you are the architect of your own perfect simulation.

56:22.260 --> 56:22.620
Exactly.

56:23.520 --> 56:29.020
Now, the standard classical philosophical reading of this experiment is that most people would

56:29.020 --> 56:35.740
decline to plug in because humans inherently value contact with actual base reality over

56:35.740 --> 56:36.700
simulated pleasure.

56:37.700 --> 56:42.660
Yes, but Flyxion offers a much more subtle, more modest reading of the dilemma.

56:43.360 --> 56:48.900
Flyxion argues that people decline the machine because they value preserving the capacity to

56:48.900 --> 56:49.400
leave.

56:49.780 --> 56:51.160
The capacity to leave?

56:51.300 --> 56:51.620
Yes.

56:51.620 --> 56:57.760
They want the ability to be corrected, surprised, and even frustrated by forces outside of the

56:57.760 --> 56:59.100
designed experience.

56:59.580 --> 57:00.100
Oh, wow.

57:00.620 --> 57:02.260
That is a massive distinction.

57:02.460 --> 57:04.700
It completely shifts the target of the critique.

57:04.980 --> 57:06.020
It changes everything.

57:06.720 --> 57:12.200
A person spending an evening deeply absorbed in a film or a community enjoying a shared digital

57:12.200 --> 57:13.880
game is not wireheaded.

57:14.580 --> 57:18.620
The ethical distinction is not about the specific content of the pleasure.

57:18.620 --> 57:25.260
The distinction is entirely about agency, dependency, and the displacement of alternatives.

57:25.800 --> 57:30.380
If the distinction is about agency, what are the actual questions we should be asking about

57:30.380 --> 57:31.300
our digital systems?

57:31.680 --> 57:33.420
We should be asking structural questions.

57:33.880 --> 57:36.460
Can the participants easily reconsider their participation?

57:37.260 --> 57:41.600
Do they have meaningful access to information and viable alternatives outside the system?

57:42.200 --> 57:47.060
Have those external alternatives been actively eroded or destroyed by the expansion of the system

57:47.060 --> 57:47.480
itself?

57:47.480 --> 57:53.360
And are there concealed material costs to this system that other hidden people are being

57:53.360 --> 57:53.940
forced to bear?

57:54.580 --> 57:56.360
It's not about whether the game is fun.

57:56.860 --> 58:01.640
It's about whether the casino locked the doors from the outside and paved over the farm to

58:01.640 --> 58:02.420
build the parking lot.

58:02.840 --> 58:03.060
Yes.

58:03.520 --> 58:09.740
And to philosophically solidify this distinction, Flyxion introduces a robust ethical framework,

58:10.300 --> 58:15.520
combining Kant's categorical imperative with Rolfe's theories of justice, specifically applying

58:15.520 --> 58:20.200
them as a universalizability test for our modern attention strategies.

58:20.400 --> 58:25.060
Kant and Rawls, we are breaking out the heavy philosophical artillery here.

58:25.400 --> 58:28.780
I love it, but we need to walk through the logic step by step.

58:29.260 --> 58:32.460
What exactly is the universalizability test?

58:32.460 --> 58:39.260
Emanuel Kant's formula of universal law essentially asks, could the core principle behind your action

58:39.260 --> 58:45.660
be adopted as a universal law for everyone, without creating a logical contradiction that

58:45.660 --> 58:46.900
defeats the action itself?

58:47.140 --> 58:47.940
Okay, I follow.

58:48.500 --> 58:53.940
And John Rawls adds a layer of fairness, asking if those principles could be reasonably justified

58:53.940 --> 58:56.320
to all the people who would be affected by them.

58:57.060 --> 59:01.540
Flyxion applies this combined test to two very different maxims for how we communicate

59:01.540 --> 59:02.160
in the world.

59:02.680 --> 59:04.320
Let's test the maxims.

59:04.960 --> 59:06.100
Give me the first one.

59:07.000 --> 59:10.240
Maxim one is, communicate what you can contribute.

59:11.120 --> 59:14.920
If I am an electrician, I communicate that I can fix wiring.

59:15.400 --> 59:17.140
Can this maxim be universalized?

59:17.780 --> 59:18.240
Let's see.

59:18.240 --> 59:24.200
If everyone in society adopts the principle, communicate what you can contribute, it doesn't

59:24.200 --> 59:25.200
create a contradiction.

59:25.940 --> 59:30.260
In fact, a society just becomes incredibly efficient at matching skills to needs.

59:30.720 --> 59:31.940
It's positive sum.

59:32.760 --> 59:36.780
The success of my communication doesn't depend on someone else failing to communicate.

59:37.600 --> 59:39.740
So, yes, it passes the test.

59:40.340 --> 59:40.820
Precisely.

59:41.820 --> 59:47.560
Now apply the test to maxim two, which is the foundational operating logic of the modern

59:47.560 --> 59:48.600
attention economy.

59:49.580 --> 59:52.300
Continually act to capture more attention than your competitors.

59:53.160 --> 59:54.420
Can this be universalized?

59:55.200 --> 59:55.940
Let me think.

59:55.940 --> 01:00:00.100
If everyone adopts the rule, I must capture more attention than the average person, it

01:00:00.100 --> 01:00:01.060
instantly collapses.

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:03.180
It is a mathematical impossibility.

01:00:03.440 --> 01:00:05.920
By definition, not everyone can be above average.

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:08.560
Not everyone can capture more attention than everyone else.

01:00:08.780 --> 01:00:08.840
Right.

01:00:09.220 --> 01:00:15.480
For me to win the zero-sum game of attention, I mathematically require an army of losers who

01:00:15.480 --> 01:00:18.260
give me their attention but receive none in return.

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:18.880
Exactly.

01:00:19.180 --> 01:00:20.220
It fails the test.

01:00:20.220 --> 01:00:22.620
It's not just that the system is highly competitive.

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:29.680
It's that presenting positional zero-sum success as a general solution for everyone, which is

01:00:29.680 --> 01:00:33.380
what modern hustle culture does, is fundamentally deceitful.

01:00:33.580 --> 01:00:34.180
Wow.

01:00:34.440 --> 01:00:40.480
Positional success explicitly relies on the unexhausted attention and the hidden sustaining labor of

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:40.780
others.

01:00:41.360 --> 01:00:45.800
Therefore, it cannot serve as a fair general term of social cooperation.

01:00:46.740 --> 01:00:50.940
So to tie a bow on the paternalism objection, the issue is not that you are playing a video

01:00:50.940 --> 01:00:51.180
game.

01:00:51.580 --> 01:00:57.640
The issue is when an institution, a platform, or an economy is intentionally engineered to

01:00:57.640 --> 01:01:02.800
erode your social and material ability to ever choose not to engage with it.

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:03.500
Yes.

01:01:03.980 --> 01:01:08.620
The true danger of wireheading is when the system structurally destroys the capacity for

01:01:08.620 --> 01:01:09.040
correction.

01:01:09.040 --> 01:01:14.360
Which brings us to the final, and arguably most urgent, section of Flyxion's paper.

01:01:15.180 --> 01:01:20.860
If the gap between reality and the metric is structural, it cannot be fixed by moral exhortation.

01:01:21.440 --> 01:01:25.220
You cannot just yell at people to, be better, or log off.

01:01:25.840 --> 01:01:28.620
We have to physically alter the design of our environments.

01:01:29.140 --> 01:01:29.400
Okay.

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:30.640
How do we do that?

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:36.840
Flyxion lays out four specific design questions and three absolute conditions for designing systems

01:01:36.840 --> 01:01:38.380
that can actually correct themselves.

01:01:38.380 --> 01:01:40.780
Let's go through the four design questions first.

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:44.040
How do we keep rewards answerable to their true purposes?

01:01:44.040 --> 01:01:49.820
The first design intervention is securing protected time for learning and maintenance away from

01:01:49.820 --> 01:01:50.780
continuous measurement.

01:01:50.980 --> 01:01:54.400
This makes perfect sense based on the math model we discussed earlier.

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:59.180
If you constantly measure a researcher by how many papers they publish every single month,

01:01:59.300 --> 01:02:02.540
you are forcing their parameter C into the red zone.

01:02:02.540 --> 01:02:07.420
They will never tackle a complex 10-year problem that might lead to a cure for a disease.

01:02:07.600 --> 01:02:10.380
They will just publish shallow incremental garbage.

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:15.180
We have to physically insulate a portion of human effort from the gaze of the dashboard.

01:02:15.320 --> 01:02:15.680
Exactly.

01:02:16.140 --> 01:02:17.260
What is the second one?

01:02:17.260 --> 01:02:19.760
The second design question is closely related.

01:02:20.400 --> 01:02:24.340
Providing institutional support for long research horizons.

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:29.980
This means designing funding and employment structures that explicitly delay the need for

01:02:29.980 --> 01:02:36.320
outputs, allowing individuals to pursue deep, unmapped questions that do not yield a steady,

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:38.860
measurable stream of intermediate results.

01:02:39.300 --> 01:02:42.220
Giving people the breathing room to do the deep work.

01:02:42.220 --> 01:02:44.160
What is the third design intervention?

01:02:45.020 --> 01:02:48.760
The third is ensuring robust opportunities for exit.

01:02:49.580 --> 01:02:54.500
Meaning, we must design systems that make it genuinely, frictionlessly easy to leave.

01:02:55.060 --> 01:02:59.200
If an organization makes it easy to leave, then the fact that people choose to stay

01:02:59.200 --> 01:03:02.420
becomes a true, uncorrupted signal of quality.

01:03:02.980 --> 01:03:04.860
But the paper notes the difficulty here, right?

01:03:04.980 --> 01:03:09.640
Because an organization has a massive short-term financial incentive to resist this.

01:03:09.640 --> 01:03:15.440
Making exit easy might reduce the system's quarterly revenue, even if it saves the system

01:03:15.440 --> 01:03:16.720
from long-term collapse.

01:03:17.360 --> 01:03:21.340
Yes, which is why it requires structural enforcement, not just corporate goodwill.

01:03:22.180 --> 01:03:27.140
Finally, the fourth design question involves creating forms of recognition that do not require

01:03:27.140 --> 01:03:29.100
permanent self-advertisement.

01:03:29.620 --> 01:03:30.740
I think about this one a lot.

01:03:31.120 --> 01:03:35.060
How do we recognize that someone is brilliant at their job without forcing them to become

01:03:35.060 --> 01:03:36.840
a full-time LinkedIn influencer?

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:40.860
Flyxion suggests revitalizing alternative mechanisms of trust.

01:03:41.660 --> 01:03:47.260
Peer recognition within small guilds, apprenticeship lineages where a master vouches for a student,

01:03:47.940 --> 01:03:52.000
and maintaining long-term records of physical, maintained work.

01:03:52.360 --> 01:03:52.860
I like that.

01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:59.000
These mechanisms can acknowledge deep contributions without demanding a continuous, noisy public

01:03:59.000 --> 01:03:59.460
presence.

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:05.440
However, the paper realistically notes that these mechanisms depend on highly stable communities

01:04:05.440 --> 01:04:09.780
with shared standards, which are themselves increasingly hard to maintain today.

01:04:10.620 --> 01:04:14.940
Now, beyond those four areas of design, Flyxion draws a hard line in the sand.

01:04:15.640 --> 01:04:20.280
The paper states that there are three absolute, non-negotiable conditions for correction.

01:04:20.720 --> 01:04:25.380
If a system does not possess these three things, it is mathematically incapable of fixing itself.

01:04:25.380 --> 01:04:28.060
These are the bedrock of viability.

01:04:28.920 --> 01:04:31.840
Condition one, failure must be observable.

01:04:32.600 --> 01:04:36.880
The discrepancy between the metric signal and the physical reality must be visible to

01:04:36.880 --> 01:04:38.440
the people inside the system.

01:04:39.080 --> 01:04:41.300
The minority reports must not be purged.

01:04:42.280 --> 01:04:43.760
Okay, we have to be able to see the gap.

01:04:44.260 --> 01:04:44.940
Condition two.

01:04:45.580 --> 01:04:47.920
Observers must be able to make an informed assessment.

01:04:48.640 --> 01:04:50.900
It is not enough merely to see a failure.

01:04:50.900 --> 01:04:56.100
The observers must have the education, the context, and the access to data required to

01:04:56.100 --> 01:05:00.120
understand what they are seeing and accurately diagnose its root cause.

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:02.320
Okay, so observation and diagnosis.

01:05:03.120 --> 01:05:04.380
And the third condition?

01:05:04.920 --> 01:05:09.240
The third condition is the hardest part, and it is where most modern institutions fail.

01:05:09.740 --> 01:05:12.560
There must be an effective route to intervention.

01:05:13.360 --> 01:05:19.160
The contrary evidence, once observed and diagnosed, must actually possess the mechanical power to alter

01:05:19.160 --> 01:05:20.060
what the system does.

01:05:20.060 --> 01:05:24.240
Let me make sure I understand the tragedy of lacking this third condition.

01:05:24.980 --> 01:05:25.880
I can see the failure.

01:05:26.200 --> 01:05:29.880
I have the data to perfectly diagnose exactly why the failure is happening.

01:05:30.360 --> 01:05:35.080
But if I have no structural power to pull a lever and force the system to change course,

01:05:35.520 --> 01:05:36.820
my knowledge is completely useless.

01:05:37.180 --> 01:05:39.860
I am just a hostage with great analytical skills.

01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:41.000
Exactly.

01:05:41.940 --> 01:05:43.640
Fliath points out a terrifying dynamic.

01:05:44.280 --> 01:05:49.000
A system that lacks an effective route to intervention might actually publish its own failures loudly.

01:05:49.000 --> 01:05:54.300
It might acknowledge its dysfunction completely openly, but because there is no mechanism for

01:05:54.300 --> 01:05:57.400
the participants to alter the arrangement, nothing will change.

01:05:57.400 --> 01:06:01.180
We are right back to Adam Curtis's hyper-normalization.

01:06:01.180 --> 01:06:03.100
We all know the system is broken.

01:06:03.320 --> 01:06:04.880
The system knows it's broken.

01:06:05.140 --> 01:06:07.900
It even publishes reports about how broken it is.

01:06:08.100 --> 01:06:10.160
But we cannot coordinate an intervention.

01:06:11.160 --> 01:06:15.760
Awareness without coordination simply leads to a paralyzed state of acknowledged dysfunction.

01:06:15.760 --> 01:06:18.440
It is like, imagine being trapped in a house.

01:06:19.120 --> 01:06:20.340
The smoke detector goes off.

01:06:20.600 --> 01:06:21.640
It is working flawlessly.

01:06:21.860 --> 01:06:26.600
It has accurately observed the failure, the fire, and diagnosed the problem.

01:06:26.860 --> 01:06:28.160
It is screaming at you.

01:06:28.400 --> 01:06:28.880
Right.

01:06:29.360 --> 01:06:33.320
But the volume dial on the detector is broken, so you can't turn it down to think.

01:06:33.900 --> 01:06:38.520
And worse, the door to the house is deadbolted from the outside, and you don't have the key.

01:06:38.520 --> 01:06:43.700
The dashboard is technically working perfectly, but you are still going to burn to death because

01:06:43.700 --> 01:06:45.860
you have absolutely no route to intervention.

01:06:46.740 --> 01:06:49.620
A harrowing but structurally perfect analogy.

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:52.780
Observation alone does not amount to correction.

01:06:53.520 --> 01:06:57.520
We must have the capacity to coordinate our efforts and physically alter the arrangements

01:06:57.520 --> 01:06:59.340
we recognize as defective.

01:06:59.660 --> 01:07:02.000
Without that, the metric is just a siren.

01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:05.880
Which brings us to the end of this incredible journey.

01:07:06.320 --> 01:07:08.700
We have covered a massive amount of ground today.

01:07:09.300 --> 01:07:14.340
We've gone from the tragedy of the wire-headed rat pressing the lever for a shock, to the

01:07:14.340 --> 01:07:19.880
mathematical reality of positional signaling, to the absurdity of the universal broadcaster,

01:07:20.440 --> 01:07:25.480
to didactic paper robots struggling with trousers, and finally, to the pluralistic ignorance

01:07:25.480 --> 01:07:29.000
of a hyper-normalized society that cannot pull the emergency brake.

01:07:29.000 --> 01:07:32.000
It is a vast, terrained map.

01:07:32.540 --> 01:07:37.980
But the central through-line Flyxion provides is incredibly clear and deeply urgent.

01:07:38.260 --> 01:07:43.460
As we synthesize all of this, I think the ultimate danger that this paper is warning us about

01:07:43.460 --> 01:07:46.240
is much subtler than a sci-fi dystopia.

01:07:46.940 --> 01:07:49.560
The danger is not that we take pleasure in a digital world.

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:53.560
It is not that we enjoy screens or want to be recognized for our work.

01:07:53.560 --> 01:08:00.880
The true danger is the progressive, silent, unrecorded loss of our very capacity

01:08:00.880 --> 01:08:04.560
to notice the difference between feeling rewarded by a metric

01:08:04.560 --> 01:08:07.600
and actually living well in the physical world.

01:08:07.980 --> 01:08:11.100
When that internal capacity to notice the difference is intact,

01:08:11.520 --> 01:08:13.880
a society can safely enjoy its entertainments,

01:08:14.300 --> 01:08:16.140
and it can aggressively pursue its ambitions.

01:08:16.140 --> 01:08:19.880
But when that capacity is destroyed by the system itself,

01:08:20.660 --> 01:08:25.420
the divergence between our recorded metrics and our physical reality widens infinitely,

01:08:25.960 --> 01:08:28.040
without ever producing an internal alarm.

01:08:28.720 --> 01:08:31.660
The dashboard tells us we are driving 60 miles an hour,

01:08:32.260 --> 01:08:34.360
while the car is sitting quietly on cinder blocks.

01:08:34.820 --> 01:08:38.140
We have to keep our rewards answerable to their true purposes.

01:08:38.620 --> 01:08:42.100
We have to reconnect the dashboard to the engine.

01:08:42.100 --> 01:08:46.800
And that leaves me with a final, somewhat provocative thought for you to mull over

01:08:46.800 --> 01:08:48.140
as you go about the rest of your day.

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:50.260
Based on everything we've explored,

01:08:50.680 --> 01:08:52.260
the systemic demand for legibility,

01:08:52.840 --> 01:08:54.940
the zero-sum contest for human attention,

01:08:55.460 --> 01:08:58.020
the metrics that slowly eat their subjects,

01:08:58.540 --> 01:09:02.920
if our modern digital systems are fundamentally designed to wirehead us

01:09:02.920 --> 01:09:07.140
by constantly demanding visible, legible, publishable performance,

01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:11.880
is the ultimate, most radical act of rebellion available to us

01:09:11.880 --> 01:09:18.320
simply choosing to do something incredibly mundane, difficult, and totally unrecordable?

01:09:19.100 --> 01:09:19.680
I love that.

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:22.340
Like planting a garden in the dark,

01:09:22.780 --> 01:09:25.480
knowing you will never, ever post a picture of it.

01:09:25.980 --> 01:09:28.180
That is a thought deeply worth holding onto.

01:09:28.440 --> 01:09:31.140
It is an act that preserves the common world.

01:09:31.740 --> 01:09:36.140
It preserves a reality that is answerable to something much deeper than the signal it produces.

01:09:36.140 --> 01:09:38.680
